    {
      "paperId": "SCOPUS-PUBLICATION:105010939141",
      "doi": "",
      "title": "A novel approach to self-adaptive digital twin performance model of turbofan engines for in-flight application",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Kim S."
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "A turbofan engine model designed for in-flight applications must ensure minimal computational cost and high reliability of results. However, traditional performance model adaptation methods based on physical models face challenges in-flight environments due to their high computational resource demands and the potential instability of numerical methods that rely heavily on initial conditions. This study addresses these limitations through a novel approach. This study attempted to use a machine learning (ML) model of a turbofan engine using neural networks to adapt to performance variations based on measurement data from flight conditions. The construction of the ML model is divided into three parts. In the first part, engine data were generated using a physics-based engine model that accounts for operating conditions and component adaptation factors (AFs). Additionally, two ML models were developed: one for calculating AFs based on operating conditions and sensor data, and another for predicting the engine performance using the operating conditions and component AFs. In the second part, the component AFs were calculated using measured parameters, and an additional ML model was developed to calculate the component AFs from the operating conditions. In the third part, the final engine model was constructed by integrating the ML models. The ML engine model, in conjunction with operating conditions, can predict the engine performance and sensor values. The self-adaptation of the performance model is achievable through the processes of the first and second parts. During the training phase, the model's maximum prediction errors for critical parameters such as the compressor rotor speed and fan turbine inlet temperature were 0.61 % and 0.94 %, respectively. This ML-driven approach outperforms traditional optimization techniques, offering faster and more reliable performance monitoring. Validation tests further confirm the model's robustness, with average prediction errors for key parameters as low as 0.34 %, demonstrating its potential for practical applications in aircraft engines.",
